            <ArticleTitle>Occupational asthma after exposure to plaster casts containing methylene diphenyl diisocyanate.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>432-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The case of a 34-year-old female nurse is presented. She worked in an accident and emergency department in a district general hospital, with methylene diphenyl diisocyanate (MDI)-containing synthetic plaster casts. She worked with MDI on a daily basis for 4 years. She was out of the department for 1 year and on her return developed cough, wheeze and dyspnoea within 5 min of exposure to MDI- containing synthetic casts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A bronchial provocation test was performed and confirmed an early asthmatic response.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was a 39% decrease in the forced expiratory volume in 1 s 15 min after exposure, which required the administration of a bronchodilator on two occasions. The patient has subsequently avoided MDI-containing synthetic plaster casts and has experienced no further respiratory symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This case illustrates that respiratory sensitization can occur as a result of exposure to MDI-containing synthetic casts and highlights the need for vigilance when health care workers are using isocyanate-containing synthetic casts.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
